//! Canonical test vectors locking the byte output of every ihi primitive.
//!
//! These tests are the cross-implementation contract — if a Rust ihi consumer, a future C/JS/wasm port, PIPE silicon, or anyone else claims to implement ihi, they must produce these exact bytes for these exact inputs. Any future change to ihi, its dependencies (blake3, sha2, sha3, i256), or rustc that alters output bytes will fail the test immediately and loudly.
//!
//! Vectors were generated by `cargo run --release --example gen_test_vectors` against ihi v0.0.42. Re-running the generator should always produce identical output; if it doesn't, something silent broke determinism — diagnose before changing the asserts.
//!
//! `chaos_amp` test vectors additionally serve as the silicon co-verification contract — PIPE's Verilog at `/mnt/Octopus/Code/pipe/rtl/chaos_amp_v2.v` must produce these exact 48 output bytes for these exact 64 input bytes. Software-silicon byte divergence anywhere here is a unification break.
//!
//! Diversity rationale: tests cover (a) the empty input edge case, (b) single bytes at both extremes (0x00 and 0xFF) to catch alignment / boundary bugs, (c) typical short and longer inputs, (d) UTF-8 multi-byte sequences (accented Latin, CJK, emoji) for unicode-byte handling, (e) inputs that start with the old VSF type marker bytes (`l`, `x`, `a`) to confirm the proof is byte-independent of any prior VSF-encoded path, and (f) repeating-byte patterns to catch collision-resistance regressions.
//!
//! The handle_proof / handle_to_proof / handle_to_filename tests run the ~1s memory-hard PoW per vector — full suite takes roughly ~13s in `--release`. They are NOT `#[ignore]`d because catching a determinism break is the entire point of having them.

use blake3;
use ihi::{
    chaos_amp, handle_proof, handle_to_filename, handle_to_proof, proof_to_filename, smear_hash,
    smear_hash_parts, spaghettify,
};

